// Cantor pairing — a clean reversible simulation of the bijection N x N -> N.
//
//   pair(x,y) = (x+y)(x+y+1)/2 + y
//
// computed with pure expressions (binary +, *, / = the verified cores' BAdd/
// BMul/BDiv) into a fresh cell z, accumulated with `+=`. The product of two
// consecutive integers is even, so the `/2` is exact. No reversible `*=`/`/=`
// is needed (the cores lack those), so the very same program is checked by BOTH
// extracted interpreters — vjanus (frame core) and driverar (flat core) —
// against PyJanus. x,y are read-only, so `uncall pair` subtracts z back to 0:
// the map (x,y) |-> z is injective and cleanly invertible.
//
// (The inverse-from-z-alone unpairing needs a triangular-root search — a sqrt-
// style reversible loop, cf. sqrt.ja — and is left as a follow-up; see
// docs/reversible-pearls.md.)  Here pair(3,5) = 8*9/2 + 5 = 41.

procedure pair(int x, int y, int z)
    z += (x + y) * (x + y + 1) / 2 + y

procedure main()
    int x
    int y
    int z
    x += 3
    y += 5
    call pair(x, y, z)
